/* data/learn-c/chem-12-01.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/chem-12-01.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["chem-12-01"] = {"1.1":{"s":"Matter is found as solid, liquid or gas. In a solid the particles are held in fixed positions and can only vibrate, so a solid has a fixed shape and volume. This chapter asks how the particles are arranged, how tightly they pack, what defects a real crystal has, and how arrangement decides electrical and magnetic behaviour.","x":["Matter around us exists as solid, liquid or gas. In a solid, the particles (atoms, ions or molecules) are close together and held in fixed positions. They can only vibrate about those positions. That is why a solid has a definite shape and volume.","This chapter studies the way the particles are arranged in a solid, and how this decides the properties: hardness, melting point, electrical conduction and magnetism. It also explains simple numerical problems about unit cells, density and packing.","The key ideas, in order: types of solids, the unit cell and the three cubic lattices, close packing and voids, packing efficiency, defects, and the electrical and magnetic behaviour of solids."],"e":"Diamond and graphite are both pure carbon, but they behave very differently. The difference is only in how the carbon atoms are arranged, which shows why structure matters."},"1.2":{"s":"Crystalline solids have their particles arranged in a regular pattern that repeats in three dimensions; they melt sharply at one temperature and give clean cleavage. Amorphous solids, like glass and rubber, have only short-range order and soften over a range of temperature; they are really supercooled liquids. Isomorphous substances have the same crystal shape with different chemicals; a polymorphic substance has more than one crystal form.","w":["A crystalline solid has a sharp melting point; an amorphous solid softens over a range."],"x":["Solids are of two broad kinds. In a crystalline solid the particles are arranged in a regular pattern that repeats over long distances (long-range order). Examples are sodium chloride, quartz, diamond and metals. They have a sharp melting point and a definite heat of fusion. They are anisotropic: some properties, such as refractive index or conductivity, depend on the direction in which they are measured.","In an amorphous solid, the particles are arranged without long-range order, only in small, short-range patches. Glass, rubber, plastics and gels are examples. They soften slowly over a range of temperature rather than melting sharply, and they are isotropic. They are also called supercooled liquids, because they flow very slowly over a very long time.","A few things to remember. When an amorphous solid breaks, it gives irregular pieces with curved surfaces, while a crystal splits along flat planes (cleavage). Quartz (crystalline) and quartz glass (amorphous) have the same chemical formula SiO₂. Most solids you meet in a laboratory are crystalline, and many are polycrystalline: made of many tiny crystals."],"e":"Heat a glass rod and it softens gradually, so you can bend it. Heat a crystal of ice and it stays solid until 0 °C, then melts at that exact temperature."},"1.3":{"s":"Crystalline solids are grouped by the particles and forces in them. Ionic crystals have ions held by electrostatic attraction (hard, brittle, conduct when molten). Covalent network crystals, such as diamond and quartz, are very hard with very high melting points. Molecular crystals have molecules held by weak forces (soft, low melting point). Metallic crystals have cations in a sea of electrons (conduct, malleable).","w":["Ionic solids do not conduct as solids, only when molten or dissolved. Metals conduct in the solid state."],"x":["Crystalline solids are classified by the kind of particles and the forces that hold them together. There are four classes.","Ionic solids (NaCl, KCl, MgO): positive and negative ions held by strong electrostatic attraction. They are hard and brittle, have high melting points, do not conduct as solids (the ions are fixed), and conduct when molten or dissolved.","Covalent or network solids (diamond, silicon carbide, quartz): atoms joined by covalent bonds through the whole crystal. They are very hard, have very high melting points and are poor conductors. Graphite is an exception: it has layers, and its free electrons let it conduct.","Molecular solids (ice, dry ice, iodine, sugar): molecules held by weak forces (van der Waals forces, dipole attraction or hydrogen bonds). They are soft, have low melting points and are non-conductors.","Metallic solids (Cu, Fe, Na): positive metal ions in a sea of freely moving electrons. They are good conductors of heat and electricity, malleable and ductile, with a shiny surface."],"e":"Sodium chloride (ionic) melts at 801 °C and conducts when molten. Dry ice (molecular) sublimes at −78 °C. Diamond (network) melts above 3500 °C."},"1.4":{"s":"A lattice is an array of points; attaching the basis (a particle or a group of particles) to every point gives the crystal. The smallest repeating part of it is the unit cell. A unit cell can be primitive (particles at corners only), body-centred, face-centred or base-centred. Only fourteen different lattices, the Bravais lattices, are possible. They fall into seven crystal systems: cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, monoclinic, triclinic, rhombohedral and hexagonal.","w":["There are 7 crystal systems but 14 lattices; the cubic system has three of them (simple, body-centred, face-centred)."],"x":["In a crystal the particles sit at definite points. Imagine each particle as a point: the set of points is the crystal lattice, or space lattice. It repeats the same pattern in all three directions.","The unit cell is the smallest portion of the lattice which, repeated in all three directions, gives the whole crystal. It is described by three edge lengths a, b and c, and three angles α, β and γ between them. There are seven crystal systems (cubic, tetragonal, orthorhombic, monoclinic, triclinic, hexagonal and rhombohedral).","A primitive unit cell has particles only at its corners. A centred unit cell has extra particles inside: body-centred (one particle at the centre of the cell), face-centred (one at the centre of each face) or end-centred (at the centres of two opposite faces).","Particles on the corners, faces or edges are shared with neighbouring cells, so only a fraction belongs to a given cell. A corner atom is shared by 8 cells (1/8 each), a face atom by 2 cells (1/2 each), an edge atom by 4 cells (1/4 each), and a body-centre atom belongs to one cell only."],"e":"A cell with an atom at each of the 8 corners and one at the centre of each of the 6 faces has 8 × 1/8 + 6 × 1/2 = 4 atoms. This is the face-centred cubic cell."},"1.5":{"s":"The cubic system has three unit cells: simple (particles at the eight corners), body-centred (corners and the centre) and face-centred (corners and the centre of each face). A corner particle is shared by eight cells and contributes one eighth, a face particle is shared by two cells and contributes one half, and a body-centre particle belongs wholly to its cell.","f":[{"x":"n = 8*(1/8) = 1 (sc), n = 8*(1/8) + 1 = 2 (bcc), n = 8*(1/8) + 6*(1/2) = 4 (fcc)","n":"Particles per unit cell: corners count 1/8 each, face centres 1/2 each, the body centre 1.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.5, p.8"}],"w":["Count the share of each particle: corner 1/8, face 1/2, edge 1/4, inside 1."],"x":["The cubic system is the simplest, with a = b = c and all angles 90°. It has three types of unit cell. In each of them you can count the atoms per cell, the coordination number and the relation between the edge a and the atomic radius r.","Simple (primitive) cubic: atoms at the 8 corners. Atoms per cell = 8 × 1/8 = 1. Coordination number = 6. The atoms touch along the edge, so a = 2r.","Body-centred cubic (BCC): corners plus one atom at the centre. Atoms per cell = 1 + 1 = 2. Coordination number = 8. The atoms touch along the body diagonal, which has length a√3, so a√3 = 4r and a = 4r/√3.","Face-centred cubic (FCC): corners plus the centre of each face. Atoms per cell = 4. Coordination number = 12. The atoms touch along the face diagonal, which has length a√2, so a√2 = 4r and a = 2√2 r.","The coordination number is the number of nearest neighbours of a particle. Iron, sodium and potassium (at room temperature) are BCC. Copper, silver, gold and aluminium are FCC. Polonium is one of the very few elements that are simple cubic."],"e":"Copper is FCC with a = 361 pm. Its atomic radius is r = a/(2√2) = 361/2.828 = 127.6 pm."},"1.6":{"s":"Particles are packed as close as possible. In two dimensions, a square arrangement has 4 neighbours and a hexagonal arrangement 6. Stacking hexagonal layers as ABAB… gives hexagonal close packing (hcp); stacking as ABCABC… gives cubic close packing (ccp), the same as fcc. In both, each sphere touches 12 others. For N particles there are N octahedral voids and 2N tetrahedral voids.","f":[{"x":"octahedral voids = N, tetrahedral voids = 2*N","n":"In hcp and ccp, for N particles in the packing.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.6, p.11"}],"w":["Coordination number is 12 in both hcp and ccp, but the stacking is ABAB… in hcp and ABCABC… in ccp."],"x":["Think of atoms as hard spheres. To pack them as closely as possible, first lay them in a flat layer so that each sphere touches six others. This is a close-packed layer (hexagonal arrangement, layer A).","To build the second layer (B), place spheres in the hollows of the first. The first layer has two sets of hollows, and the second layer can only use one set. For the third layer there are two choices.","If the third layer lies exactly above the first (ABAB...), the structure is hexagonal close packing (hcp). If the third layer sits over the hollows that were left uncovered (ABCABC...), it is cubic close packing (ccp), whose unit cell is the face-centred cubic one. In both, each sphere has 12 neighbours, and 74% of the space is filled.","Between the spheres there are empty spaces called voids. A tetrahedral void is surrounded by 4 spheres and an octahedral void by 6. In a close-packed structure of N spheres there are 2N tetrahedral voids and N octahedral voids. The octahedral void is larger. In ionic solids, the smaller ions often sit in these voids."],"e":"In 1 mole of ccp atoms (6.022 × 10²³ atoms) there are 1.2 × 10²⁴ tetrahedral voids and 6.022 × 10²³ octahedral voids."},"1.7":{"s":"Packing efficiency is the percentage of the unit cell volume occupied by the particles. It is found from the volume of the particles in one cell divided by the volume a³ of the cell. It is 52.4% for simple cubic, 68% for body-centred cubic and 74% for face-centred cubic and hcp. The density of a crystal follows from the particles per cell, the molar mass and the edge length.","f":[{"x":"Packing efficiency = (volume occupied by particles in a unit cell)/(volume of the unit cell) × 100","n":"The definition.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.11"},{"x":"a = 2*r; efficiency = 52.4 %","n":"Simple cubic: the particles touch along the edge of the cell.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.11"},{"x":"r = sqrt(3)*a/4; efficiency = 68 %","n":"Body-centred cubic: the particles touch along the body diagonal.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.12"},{"x":"r = a/(2*sqrt(2)); efficiency = 74 %","n":"Face-centred cubic and ccp: the particles touch along the face diagonal.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.13"},{"x":"rho = (n/a^3)*(M/N_A)","n":"Density of a crystal: n = particles per unit cell, a = edge length, M = molar mass, N_A = Avogadro constant.","r":"Balbharati Chemistry XII, 1.7, p.14"}],"w":["The face-diagonal is 4r in fcc and the body-diagonal is 4r in bcc: that is where r = a/(2√2) and r = √3a/4 come from.","Convert the edge length to cm (1 pm = 10⁻¹⁰ cm) before using it in the density."],"x":["Packing efficiency is the percentage of the volume of the unit cell that is actually filled by the atoms: (volume of atoms in the cell / volume of the cell) × 100. The rest is empty space.","FCC: 4 atoms, each of volume (4/3)πr³, in a cell of volume a³ = (2√2 r)³ = 16√2 r³. Efficiency = 4 × (4/3)πr³/(16√2 r³) × 100 = π/(3√2) × 100 = 74%. The hcp structure gives the same value.","BCC: 2 atoms in a cell of volume a³ = (4r/√3)³. Efficiency = 2 × (4/3)πr³/(64r³/(3√3)) × 100 = π√3/8 × 100 = 68%.","Simple cubic: 1 atom in a cell of volume (2r)³ = 8r³. Efficiency = (4/3)πr³/8r³ × 100 = π/6 × 100 = 52.4%.","So the order, from lowest to highest, is simple cubic (52.4%), then BCC (68%), then FCC or hcp (74%). The empty fraction is 26% for FCC, 32% for BCC and 47.6% for simple cubic."],"e":"A metal that is FCC is denser than the same atoms packed BCC. A crystal changing from BCC to FCC on cooling shrinks slightly, as happens with iron near 910 °C."},"1.8":{"s":"Real crystals have defects. In a vacancy defect a particle is missing from its site and the density falls. In a self-interstitial defect an extra particle enters the lattice and the density rises; if a lattice particle only shifts to an interstitial site, the density is unchanged. A Schottky defect (NaCl) is a missing cation-anion pair: density falls. A Frenkel defect (ZnS) is a cation moved to an interstitial site: density unchanged.","w":["Schottky defect: density decreases. Frenkel defect: density unchanged. Both keep the crystal electrically neutral.","Frenkel defects need a small cation and a large anion, so they are not found in the alkali metal halides."],"x":["A perfect crystal, with every particle in its place, does not exist at any temperature above absolute zero. Real crystals have defects, which are irregularities in the arrangement. Defects change the properties: density, colour, conductivity.","Point defects are faults at single lattice points. In stoichiometric ionic crystals (the ratio of ions is unchanged) there are two types. The Schottky defect is a pair of missing ions, one cation and one anion, so the crystal stays neutral. It lowers the density. It is found in compounds with ions of similar size, such as NaCl, KCl and CsCl. The Frenkel defect is an ion, usually the smaller cation, moving from its normal site into an interstitial site. The density stays the same. It is found when the cation is much smaller than the anion, as in AgBr and ZnS.","In non-stoichiometric crystals the ratio of ions is changed. A metal excess defect arises when there are anionic vacancies occupied by electrons (F-centres). They give colour: NaCl heated in sodium vapour turns yellow. A metal deficiency defect arises when a cation is missing and a neighbouring one has a higher charge to keep the crystal neutral, as in FeO, which is really Fe₀.₉₅O.","Impurity defects occur when a foreign ion takes the place of another. Adding a little SrCl₂ to NaCl, for example, replaces two Na⁺ by one Sr²⁺ and leaves a cationic vacancy."],"e":"A crystal of AgBr shows the Frenkel defect. Its density is unchanged. A crystal of NaCl with Schottky defects has a density a little lower than the perfect crystal."},"1.9":{"s":"Solids differ greatly in electrical conductivity. In the band theory, the overlap of many atomic orbitals gives a valence band and a conduction band. In a conductor the bands overlap or the conduction band is partly filled. In an insulator a large forbidden gap separates a full valence band from an empty conduction band. In a semiconductor the gap is small. Doping a semiconductor with a group 15 element gives n-type, with a group 13 element p-type.","w":["Metals conduct less when heated; semiconductors conduct more, because heat lifts electrons across the small gap."],"x":["Solids differ greatly in how well they conduct electricity. Conductors (metals) have conductivity of 10⁴ to 10⁷ S/m. Insulators (diamond, rubber) are below 10⁻¹⁰ S/m. Semiconductors (silicon, germanium) lie in between, 10⁻⁶ to 10⁴ S/m.","Band theory explains this. In a solid the atomic energy levels merge into bands. The valence band is the highest band occupied by electrons, and the conduction band above it is where electrons can move freely. In a conductor the bands overlap or the valence band is partly filled. In an insulator there is a large energy gap between them, and electrons cannot jump across. In a semiconductor the gap is small (about 1 eV for silicon), so a few electrons are excited across at room temperature.","The conductivity of a metal falls as the temperature rises, since the vibrating ions scatter the electrons more. That of a semiconductor rises with temperature, since more electrons cross the gap.","Doping makes semiconductors useful. Adding a group 15 element (As, P) to silicon gives an n-type semiconductor: the extra fifth electron moves freely. Adding a group 13 element (B, Al, Ga) gives a p-type semiconductor: each atom leaves a hole that acts as a positive carrier. Junctions of the two make diodes and transistors."],"e":"Pure silicon conducts poorly. Adding about one atom of arsenic per million of silicon increases its conductivity by orders of magnitude."},"1.10":{"s":"Solids respond to a magnetic field in different ways. Diamagnetic substances have all their electrons paired and are weakly repelled. Paramagnetic substances have unpaired electrons and are weakly attracted. In a ferromagnetic substance the unpaired electrons in a region align in the same direction and the substance is strongly attracted and can be magnetised.","x":["Magnetic behaviour comes from electrons. Each electron has a spin and an orbital motion, and each acts as a tiny magnet. Whether a substance is magnetic depends on whether these small magnets cancel.","Diamagnetic substances have all their electrons paired, so the effects cancel. They are weakly repelled by a magnetic field. Examples: NaCl, water, benzene, copper metal. Paramagnetic substances have unpaired electrons. They are weakly attracted by a field and lose the effect when it is removed. Examples: O₂, Cu²⁺, Fe³⁺.","In ferromagnetic substances (Fe, Co, Ni, CrO₂) the magnetic moments of many atoms line up the same way in domains. They are strongly attracted and stay magnetised after the field is removed (permanent magnets). Heating above the Curie temperature destroys the order and they become paramagnetic.","In antiferromagnetic substances (MnO) the moments line up in opposite directions and cancel, giving zero net magnetism. In ferrimagnetic substances (Fe₃O₄, ferrites) they are opposite but unequal, so there is a small net moment. Ferrimagnetic substances are used in memory devices."],"e":"Liquid oxygen sticks between the poles of a strong magnet (paramagnetic), but liquid nitrogen is not attracted (diamagnetic)."}};
